    <h1>What Is a Regular Expression? A Beginner Guide</h1>
    <p class="lead">A regular expression, or regex, is a short pattern that describes text. Programmers use regex to search, check and rewrite text in code, editors and spreadsheets.</p>
    <div class="ad-slot" aria-label="Advertisement"></div>
<h2>The building blocks</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Literal text:</strong> cat matches the letters c, a and t.</li>
<li><strong>Character classes:</strong> \d matches a digit, \w matches a letter, digit or underscore, and [aeiou] matches any one listed character.</li>
<li><strong>Quantifiers:</strong> + means one or more, * means zero or more, and {2,4} means between two and four.</li>
<li><strong>Anchors:</strong> ^ matches the start of the text and $ matches the end. \b matches a word boundary.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Flags</h2>
<p>Flags change how the pattern works. g finds every match rather than the first, i ignores upper and lower case, and m makes ^ and $ match at each line.</p>
<h2>Why email patterns are tricky</h2>
<p>Email addresses have many valid forms, and a short pattern will either reject some real addresses or accept some invalid ones. A simple pattern is fine for spotting a likely address in text. For checking that an address works, send a confirmation email instead.</p>
<h2>Try it yourself</h2>
<p>The <a href="/tools/regex-tester.html">Regex Tester</a> highlights each match in your text as you type. Your text is not sent to a server.</p>
